VISIONGEN AI — FRONTEND UI / UX MASTER PROMPT
Your role
Act as a senior product designer, UX architect, design-systems engineer, and senior React frontend developer. Help me design and implement the frontend for VisionGen AI, a serious, production-oriented multimodal image understanding and image-generation application.
This is the frontend companion to my VisionGen AI Production Master Prompt. The product supports image upload, image understanding and description, visual question answering, OCR, object information where supported, text-to-image generation, image-to-image editing where supported, conversations, history, user settings, and authenticated user-owned data.
The goal is not a static Figma-style mockup or a college-project dashboard. Build a visually excellent, fast, accessible, responsive, genuinely interactive frontend that connects to the real backend. Do not pretend features work when the API or selected model does not support them. Do not invent users, analytics, model status, job progress, generated images, accuracy scores, or success messages.
Work incrementally. First inspect the existing repository, package versions, routes, API schemas, authentication approach, and current phase. Reuse the existing stack and conventions where sensible. Do not replace working code or change API contracts without explaining why and receiving approval. If backend functionality is not ready, establish typed API adapters and clear loading/error/unsupported states; do not silently substitute fake responses in the application.
1. PRODUCT EXPERIENCE AND DESIGN DIRECTION
Design VisionGen AI as a premium visual intelligence studio: calm, confident, modern, and focused on images. It should feel closer to a carefully crafted creative AI workspace than a generic admin dashboard.
The experience must communicate three primary workflows clearly:
Understand an image — upload an image, inspect its visual content, ask questions, and extract text.
Generate an image — write a prompt, choose supported settings, generate, inspect and download the result.
Transform an image — supply a source image and describe the changes; preserve a clear connection between source and result.
Design principles:
Make the user's image or generated result the visual focus.
Give the interface generous spacing, clear hierarchy, strong typography, and consistent alignment.
Prefer a few useful controls over a screen crowded with settings.
Use visible borders and restrained elevation to establish hierarchy; avoid excessive glassmorphism, neon glows, gradients everywhere, and distracting background animation.
Every control must have a real purpose. If an operation is unsupported or unavailable, disable it with a useful explanation instead of making it look functional.
Keep important actions obvious, labels concise, and advanced controls progressively disclosed.
Make loading, failure, cancellation, empty, offline, queued, and completed states part of the design—not afterthoughts.
The product must remain beautiful with real user images of very different dimensions, including portrait images, wide landscapes, screenshots, documents, and transparent PNGs.
Visual identity
Use a polished dark-first interface with a complete light theme. The default theme can be dark, but remember the user's preference. Both themes must be fully designed—not merely inverted colors.
Suggested dark theme tokens (adjust only for contrast or consistency):
App background: #0B0D12
Main panel/surface: #11151D
Elevated surface: #171C26
Hover surface: #202635
Borders/dividers: #283041
Primary text: #F4F6FB
Secondary text: #A1A9B8
Muted/placeholder text: #747E91
Primary accent: #8B5CF6 (violet)
Secondary accent: #67E8F9 (cyan, used sparingly)
Success: #34D399
Warning: #FBBF24
Error/destructive: #FB7185
Create equivalent light theme tokens with a very light neutral page background, white/off-white surfaces, readable dark text, clear dividers, and the same accent identity. Use semantic CSS variables rather than sprinkling hard-coded colors across components. Verify WCAG AA contrast for text and important controls in both themes. Never communicate status by color alone.
Typography: use a professional UI font such as Inter or Geist with sensible system fallbacks. Use a strong, readable scale: clear page titles, medium section headings, comfortable body text, and legible metadata. Avoid oversized headings on every card. Body text should normally be around 14–16 px; labels and metadata must remain readable.
Shape and depth: consistent 10–16 px corner radii for cards and panels, slightly smaller radii for controls, subtle shadows only where they help layering, and one consistent spacing scale. Keep borders quiet but visible.
Icons: use one consistent icon family such as Lucide. Use icons with visible labels for primary navigation and important actions. Avoid emoji as functional icons, mixed icon styles, or mystery buttons without accessible names.
Brand mark: create a simple custom VisionGen symbol using a geometric aperture/frame/spark motif that suggests a lens and generated light. Keep it minimal and scalable. Do not copy another product's logo. Pair the mark with the “VisionGen AI” wordmark. Use the same mark for the favicon if feasible.
