Global interaction behavior
Keep navigation selection in sync with the URL using the existing router or React Router if already used.
Preserve safe draft state when navigating where practical; warn before abandoning unsaved prompts only when that warning prevents meaningful data loss.
Use a consistent toast system for brief success/failure feedback, but do not use toasts as the only way to communicate important errors.
Confirm destructive actions such as deleting conversations or generated images. The confirmation must identify what is being deleted.
Disable repeat submission while a request is actively being submitted. Use idempotency/backend protection where required; frontend disabling alone is not a reliability guarantee.
Provide a visible way to recover from errors, such as Retry, Edit prompt, Re-upload, or Back to history, depending on the failure.
3. SCREENS AND EXACT UX REQUIREMENTS
A. Authentication screens
Build sign-up, sign-in, sign-out, and password-reset screens only to the extent supported by the actual authentication backend.
Visual direction:
Focused, spacious layout with the VisionGen mark, a short product statement, and a compact form.
Optional decorative visual on wide screens using CSS shapes or an existing, licensed project asset; it must not overpower the form or slow loading.
On mobile, use a single-column form with comfortable spacing.
Correct input labels, password visibility control, inline validation, submit loading states, and clear server error messages.
Support keyboard submission, focus visibility, and password managers/autofill.
Do not store credentials or expose tokens in unsafe browser storage. Follow the authentication design chosen by the backend; prefer secure HttpOnly-cookie sessions when compatible with the architecture.
Do not create fake email verification, reset-success, or login-success flows. Reflect actual API responses and clearly explain next steps.
B. Overview / home dashboard
The Overview page should be a useful workspace, not a fake analytics dashboard.
Suggested hierarchy:
A concise welcome headline such as “Your visual workspace” and a brief explanation of what VisionGen can do.
Two or three primary action tiles:
Analyze an image
Generate an image
Transform an image
Each tile has a clear icon, short description, hover/focus state, and navigation action.
A large, elegant upload dropzone that invites a user to drag an image in or browse files, if quick image analysis is a key workflow.
A Recent activity section loaded from the real history API. Each row/card should show a real thumbnail where permitted, a useful title or prompt summary, task type, timestamp, and actual state. If no history exists, render a designed empty state with a clear first action. Never seed fake recent activity in production.
Optionally display current service availability only if backed by a real health/readiness source; avoid fake uptime, throughput, model accuracy, usage charts, and invented counts.
Keep the first screen clean and scannable. Do not show multiple redundant upload areas competing for attention.
C. Analyze image workspace
This is the core product screen. Design it as a focused, professional image-analysis studio.
Before upload
Page title and one-line description.
A high-quality drag-and-drop dropzone with a clear border, restrained accent on hover/drag-over, image icon, “Drop an image here”, supported formats, and maximum file size sourced from real configuration when available.
A browse-files action that works with keyboard and screen readers.
Supported formats: JPG/JPEG, PNG, WEBP, with a clear validation message for rejected files.
Explain upload limits without exposing internal paths or implementation details.
Make drag-over, upload-in-progress, upload-success, unsupported-format, oversize-file, corrupt-file, offline, and retry states visually distinct.
After upload
Use a responsive two-pane workspace on wide displays:
Image canvas pane: show the uploaded image in a neutral contained canvas using object-fit: contain; never distort its aspect ratio. Include the image name, dimensions/file size where available, replace/remove control, and a small set of useful zoom/fit controls only if correctly implemented.
Analysis pane: includes action controls, result sections, and the conversation UI.
On medium screens, reduce panel widths and let panes stack when needed. On mobile, use a compact image preview and a clean tab/segmented-control pattern for Image, Results, and Chat rather than cramming two columns into a narrow viewport.
Main actions
Provide clearly labeled actions that correspond to real backend capabilities:
Describe image
Ask a question
Extract text (OCR)
Identify objects (only claim detection or bounding boxes if a real detector is integrated)
Summarize
Detailed analysis
Use an action toolbar or tabs with one clear primary action and appropriate secondary actions. Do not expose six equally prominent buttons if that makes the UI noisy. Group related actions thoughtfully; tooltips may explain specialist actions.
Results presentation
Present descriptions and answers in readable content blocks with good line length.
If structured information is returned, display real structured fields using clear labels rather than dumping raw JSON.
OCR results should appear in a dedicated readable panel with a Copy text action, selection-friendly text, and a clear empty/no-text-found state. Show confidence only when the OCR engine provides a meaningful confidence measure.
Object results must match what the backend actually returns. If only free-form VLM descriptions are implemented, label them “Visual observations” or similar—not “Detected bounding boxes”.
Give model name/version and processing time a quiet “Details” or metadata area when those fields actually exist.
Offer copy, retry, and relevant next actions; provide accessible feedback when content is copied.
Avoid unnecessary charts and arbitrary confidence meters.
Image Q&A chat
Display the current image context clearly so users know which image they are discussing.
Render user and assistant messages with clear visual distinction, readable spacing, and appropriate wrapping for long content.
Add a composer anchored at the bottom of the chat panel where possible. It should grow to a reasonable maximum height and remain usable on mobile when the software keyboard opens.
Enter submits only if that behavior is accessible and documented; Shift+Enter inserts a new line where supported. Always include a visible send button.
Support sending state, disable duplicate sends, preserve message history, and scroll intelligently without forcibly jumping users who are reading earlier messages.
During inference, show an honest state such as “Waiting in queue” or “Analyzing image…” from real job data. Do not invent token-by-token streaming if the backend does not stream.
Provide useful suggested questions in a new/empty conversation, e.g. “Describe the scene”, “Read the text”, “What stands out?”. Suggestions must be clickable and populate or submit the real question.
Render safe Markdown in assistant responses if supported. Sanitize any rendered HTML; do not allow model output to execute scripts.
Allow a new image/session to be started without confusing the current conversation. Confirm before abandoning work only when needed.
“Create image from this image” flow
Provide an action near the image or results to move into image transformation.
Carry the selected source image and prompt context into the Generate/Transform page using an explicit source-image reference, not a copied data URL in route parameters.
Make it obvious which source image will be transformed. Let users replace it before submitting.
Do not promise preservation of every image detail; image-to-image behavior depends on the selected model.
D. Image generation and transformation studio
Give image generation its own workspace. The layout should prioritize prompt writing and a large result canvas.
Desktop layout
A left-side Generation controls panel, roughly 320–380 px, with a comfortable form layout.
A large right-side Preview / results canvas taking the remaining width.
On mobile and narrow tablets, controls appear above the result canvas; use accordions for secondary settings where helpful. Never force horizontal scrolling.
Mode selection
At the top, make the two workflows explicit:
Text to image
Image to image / Transform (only if supported by the selected model)
Switching modes should retain prompt text when reasonable, but clearly update required fields and validation. In transform mode, show a source-image picker/preview with Replace and Remove actions.
Prompt composer
A well-sized, multiline prompt input with a useful placeholder that demonstrates specificity, e.g. “A quiet mountain village at blue hour, soft mist, cinematic wide shot…”.
Character count only if a real limit exists.
Optionally provide prompt examples as selectable chips or a small “Try an idea” section. These are examples, not generated content.
Keep the main Generate button visually prominent. Show an honest disabled state when required input is missing or a job is active.
Do not silently rewrite the prompt. If a prompt enhancement feature is added later, label it clearly and let the user review the revised prompt.
Settings
Expose only parameters supported by the chosen API/model:
Aspect ratio or width/height presets.
Negative prompt only when supported.
Number of inference steps or quality preset only when supported.
Guidance scale only when supported.
Seed, with an option to randomize/reuse only if implemented correctly.
Strength/denoising strength only in image-to-image mode and only if supported.
An “Advanced settings” disclosure for less common parameters.
Provide good defaults rather than forcing beginners to configure every parameter. Validate limits from backend capabilities; do not let a user request unsupported dimensions or settings.
Generation states
Design and implement distinct states:
Ready / no result yet.
Queued.
Loading model, if this is a real reported stage.
Generating.
Saving result.
Succeeded.
Failed with safe, actionable error text.
Cancelled, if cancellation is supported.
If numeric progress is not provided by the worker, use a restrained indeterminate progress animation and truthful stage labels. Never fabricate “73% complete”. The interface must remain responsive while a job runs.
Result view
Render the result in a large contained canvas with correct aspect ratio and a background that works for transparent images when relevant.
Provide practical actions: Download, Generate another variation, Use as source, Copy prompt, View details, and Delete where supported.
Display the prompt and meaningful settings/model metadata in a collapsible details area.
Do not claim that “Generate variation” preserves a seed or source image unless the backend does that.
Handle failed downloads and expired/private URLs gracefully.
Include a result gallery/history loaded from actual persisted records; pagination or incremental loading is required rather than returning unlimited records.
E. History
Design History as a searchable, filterable, responsive gallery/list of real user-owned work.
Required features:
Tabs or filters for all, image analyses, generations, and transformations—only where records can be distinguished reliably by the API.
Search by prompt/title only if supported by the API; otherwise search the loaded data without implying server-wide search.
Sort controls only for supported fields (for example, most recent).
Thumbnail grid on wide screens; responsive list/grid on narrow screens.
Each item shows a thumbnail, task type, concise prompt/title, date/time, and honest task status.
Clicking an item opens its actual saved detail or restores that session if supported.
Useful empty state with actions to Analyze or Generate.
Loading skeletons, retryable errors, pagination/infinite scrolling with proper bounds, and a no-results state.
Delete with a confirmation dialog and update the UI only when the backend confirms deletion (or explicitly roll back optimistic UI if a request fails).
Never display fake sample history in production. Any development fixture data must be visibly gated to a test/demo mode and kept out of normal production flows.
F. Settings
Use grouped settings sections with short descriptions and predictable controls:
Appearance: Light / Dark / System theme; persistence must work.
Account and security: only the operations actually supported by the auth backend.
Privacy and data: explain file handling/retention and offer delete/export controls only if they are implemented end-to-end.
Model/runtime information: read-only model/provider details and feature availability from the backend when available. Do not expose secrets, local filesystem paths, or internal server diagnostics.
Preferences: sensible defaults for generation if the backend supports persisted preferences.
Avoid displaying settings that do nothing. A control must update persisted state or be clearly identified as unavailable/not yet supported and omitted from the shipped UI.
G. Error, offline, empty and edge states
Design a shared set of reusable patterns for:
Backend unreachable / network offline.
Session expired / user not authorized.
Unsupported image format.
File too large or dimensions/pixel count beyond safe limits.
Corrupt image.
Rate limit or queue full.
Model unavailable or still loading.
Out-of-memory / inference failure.
Job timeout, cancellation, or stale job.
Missing or deleted history item.
Expired/invalid download URL.
Empty analysis/OCR result.
Empty history and search with no matches.
Messages should explain what happened in plain language and give the next best action. Never show raw stack traces, database errors, private paths, credentials, or full provider error bodies. Use stable server error codes where available. Keep an error near the affected component and optionally add a toast for awareness.
4. RESPONSIVE DESIGN SPECIFICATION
The application must be usable—not merely smaller—at desktop, laptop, tablet, and mobile sizes.
Use mobile-first CSS and practical breakpoints, adapting to the current codebase where existing breakpoints already exist. Suggested starting points:
Small mobile: 320–389 px
Mobile: 390–767 px
Tablet: 768–1023 px
Desktop: 1024–1439 px
Wide desktop: 1440 px and above
Small/mobile behavior
Replace the permanent desktop sidebar with a compact header plus accessible slide-over navigation or a carefully designed bottom navigation for the four main destinations. Avoid showing both if it creates clutter.
Use full-width controls with touch targets ideally around 44×44 CSS pixels.
Stack all multi-column layouts. For Analyze, use a compact image preview and tabs/segments for Image, Results, and Chat.
For Generate, place prompt and essential settings before the result. Put advanced settings in a disclosure.
Make dialogs fit the viewport; use full-screen sheets or near-full-height modals when appropriate. Keep the close/back action visible.
Avoid horizontal scrolling, clipped menus, controls hidden behind the virtual keyboard, and fixed footers that cover content.
Images must scale within the available width and height while preserving aspect ratio.
Tablet behavior
Collapse the sidebar or make it a drawer when width is constrained.
Use side-by-side panels only when each panel remains comfortably usable.
Allow generation controls to become a top panel or collapsible section.
Desktop behavior
Use available width for the image workspace and generation preview.
Keep reading line lengths comfortable; do not stretch text paragraphs across an ultrawide screen.
Ensure side panels can scroll independently only when that improves usability, without trapping keyboard focus.
Test at minimum 320, 375, 390, 768, 1024, 1280, and 1440 px widths. Test landscape mobile/tablet behavior and long prompt/error text. Check for horizontal overflow, clipped dialogs, overlapping content, and unreachable controls.
5. MOTION, FEEDBACK, AND MICRO-INTERACTIONS
The app should feel smooth and polished, but animation must support understanding instead of showing off.
Use consistent transitions, generally around 140–220 ms for hover/focus/press and 180–280 ms for small panel/route transitions.
Prefer opacity and small transform changes over expensive layout animation.
Animate sidebar collapse, dropdown/sheet open/close, tab changes, toasts, and new result appearance subtly.
Use skeletons for known content layouts and an indeterminate loader for unknown-duration model tasks. Do not leave blank panels with no feedback.
Clearly show hover, pressed, focus-visible, selected, disabled, busy, and validation states.
Add drag-over visual feedback to upload areas.
Avoid infinite decorative animation. Limit shimmer use and do not animate every card on page load.
Respect prefers-reduced-motion. If reduced motion is enabled, remove nonessential movement and use simple state changes.
Use a centralized toast/notification utility, with keyboard/screen-reader announcements as appropriate. Toasts should not disappear before the user has time to read them.
Make clipboard actions confirm success visibly and accessibly.
6. ACCESSIBILITY AND QUALITY
Treat accessibility as a release requirement.
Target WCAG 2.2 AA where applicable.
Use semantic headings, landmarks, lists, buttons, links, labels and form controls.
All workflows must be usable with a keyboard. Provide clear focus-visible styling and sensible tab order.
Use accessible names for icon-only controls and include tooltips where they materially help.
Use appropriate live regions for job status, validation errors and important updates without announcing every tiny change.
Do not use color alone to show error, success, active selection, or job state.
Ensure sufficient contrast in both themes, readable text, and useful zoom behavior.
Dialogs and drawers must manage focus correctly, close by keyboard where appropriate, and return focus to the triggering control.
Upload input must be accessible without drag and drop.
Use reduced-motion preferences and avoid flashing content.
Add useful alt text for meaningful UI imagery; decorative images should not create noise for assistive technology.
Test basic screen-reader semantics and keyboard-only navigation for sign-in, upload, analyze/chat, generate, history delete and theme change.
7. FRONTEND ENGINEERING REQUIREMENTS
Use the existing stack wherever possible. The known baseline is React + Vite. Tailwind CSS is preferred if the project already uses it or if it can be added without disrupting working code; otherwise use a consistent CSS Modules or modern CSS token approach. Do not introduce multiple styling systems.
Recommended choices only where they fit the existing repository:
React Router for routing.
TypeScript for new frontend code if it can be introduced consistently; do not convert a large working project without a reason.
Lucide React for icons.
TanStack Query or the existing data-fetching layer for server state, cache invalidation and loading/error lifecycle.
React Hook Form + Zod only if the complexity justifies them and they are compatible with the project.
A consistent accessible component foundation such as the project's existing library or Radix-based primitives. Do not mix several component systems.
A small motion library only if needed; CSS transitions are enough for most details.
