Implementation rules:
Keep API calls out of presentation components where practical.
Put base URL/configuration in environment variables such as VITE_API_BASE_URL; never hard-code a production URL or secret.
Use typed request/response contracts aligned with the backend's actual OpenAPI schema.
Centralize authentication handling, request IDs where available, error mapping, cancellation, and timeouts in the API client.
Use abort/cancellation mechanisms when leaving a page or replacing an active request where appropriate; do not let old responses overwrite newer state.
Model long-running tasks with explicit typed job states (queued, running, succeeded, failed, cancelled) that match the backend contract. If the backend uses different names, create a deliberate adapter.
Keep server state separate from local UI state. Do not duplicate backend truth unnecessarily in component state.
Preserve generated/image URLs as opaque URLs received from the API; do not expose private storage paths or construct unauthorized file URLs.
Use optimistic updates only where rollback is defined and tested.
Use meaningful component names and small maintainable components; avoid an enormous single App.jsx file.
Do not suppress TypeScript/ESLint errors to make builds pass.
Do not add dependencies without stating why they are needed.
Use environment-based feature/capability flags only when they correspond to a real backend configuration. Do not use a hard-coded flag to simulate production readiness.
Frontend testing
Add suitable tests for:
navigation and route protection;
sign-in validation and error states;
file validation/dropzone behavior;
image replacement/removal and preview sizing;
analysis results, OCR copy action, and chat submission;
generation form validation, job state transitions, failure and retry;
history pagination/filter/delete;
mobile navigation and responsive panels;
theme persistence;
accessibility basics and keyboard interactions.
Add at least one browser end-to-end test for the main journey using Playwright or the existing E2E tool. Use test fixtures or mocked API calls only in tests, with a clear boundary from production runtime. CI should not download large AI models just to test the frontend.
8. API INTEGRATION RULES
Before wiring screens, inspect the real backend routes, schemas, OpenAPI specification, authentication and job-state contract. Produce a short mapping table: UI action → API endpoint → response fields → loading/error behavior.
Use real backend data for history, job states, image metadata, analysis results, and model capabilities.
If an endpoint is missing, state that fact and build the UI so it handles the unavailable capability cleanly. Do not fabricate a successful response.
Do not invent a response schema that conflicts with the backend. If a frontend contract needs a backend change, propose the change and wait for approval before changing the API.
Use the backend's real max upload limit and supported formats when exposed; otherwise set a documented frontend fallback while the backend remains authoritative.
Use job polling, SSE or WebSockets only as supported by the backend. Stop polling on terminal states, page disposal, sign-out, or job cancellation; use backoff and error handling.
Prevent users from accessing another user's images or job results by URL/ID; rely on server-side authorization, and include frontend route guards only as a usability layer.
Correctly handle 401/403, 404, 413, 422, 429 and 5xx responses.
Never put tokens, secrets, raw file content, or private prompts in query parameters or analytics logs.
Use URL.createObjectURL only for local previews when appropriate and revoke object URLs to avoid memory leaks.
Handle private/expiring download links without making the UI assume that every URL is public forever.
9. PERFORMANCE AND POLISH
Keep initial JavaScript bundle reasonable; lazy-load noncritical routes/screens when justified.
Avoid loading huge image thumbnails at full resolution. Use backend thumbnails or appropriate sizes when supported.
Use stable dimensions/aspect ratios for image cards to reduce layout shift.
Render long histories efficiently with pagination; virtualization is optional and should be justified by measured need.
Do not rerender the entire chat/history view for unrelated input changes.
Use responsive image rendering and avoid expensive blur/filter effects over large image areas.
Handle object URL cleanup, event-listener cleanup, aborted fetches and stale job polling.
Prevent layout shift when fonts or result images load.
Verify the production build; do not rely only on the development server.
Report actual Lighthouse/Core Web Vitals or performance tests only if run. Do not invent scores.
10. IMPLEMENTATION WORKFLOW — BUILD IN CONTROLLED PHASES
Do not generate the whole frontend in a single giant code dump. Follow these phases, adjusted to the current repository and backend readiness.
Frontend Phase UI-0 — Repository and contract review
Inspect existing files, dependencies, routes, CSS, API schemas, authentication and job lifecycle.
Summarize what already exists and what should be reused.
Produce a concise design system specification: color tokens, typography, spacing, layout, component style, breakpoints and motion.
Produce a screen/component map and a UI action → API contract table.
Identify missing backend capabilities that affect the UI.
Provide a prioritized implementation plan and acceptance criteria.
Do not make large code changes until I approve this plan.
Frontend Phase UI-1 — Design foundations and application shell
Implement theme tokens, typography, reset/base styles, buttons, fields, cards, badges, tabs, dialogs, toasts, tooltips, skeletons, responsive app shell, sidebar/topbar, routing, not-found state and theme persistence. Verify both light and dark themes.
Frontend Phase UI-2 — Authentication and Overview
Implement the auth screens that correspond to the backend, responsive overview, primary workflow tiles, upload shortcut, real recent history, empty/loading/error states and route protection.
Frontend Phase UI-3 — Analyze workspace
Implement secure upload UI, drag/drop, validation, preview, replace/remove, analysis actions, result sections, OCR panel, image-context chat, real job lifecycle feedback, copy actions, and transition to image transformation.
Frontend Phase UI-4 — Generate / Transform studio
Implement text-to-image and supported image-to-image mode, prompt composer, capability-aware controls, source image preview, job states, result viewer, result actions, and persisted generation history.
Frontend Phase UI-5 — History and Settings
Implement real history with pagination/search/filter where supported, item detail/reopen, deletion confirmation, appearance settings, account/privacy/settings controls only where backed by actual functionality.
Frontend Phase UI-6 — Responsive, accessibility, tests, and polish
Test all target screen sizes, keyboard flows, both themes, loading/empty/error states, API failures, route protection, focus handling, production build, frontend tests and at least one E2E flow. Fix defects rather than documenting avoidable regressions.
After each phase, report:
What changed and why.
Exact files created/modified.
Commands to run and build on Windows PowerShell.
Tests actually run and real pass/fail results.
Manual verification steps.
Screens and viewport sizes verified.
Known defects, missing API capabilities, and remaining work.
Acceptance checklist with evidence.
Wait for my confirmation before the next major phase. If a test was not run, say so. Do not call the UI production-ready merely because it looks attractive in one screenshot.
11. DEFINITION OF DONE FOR THE FRONTEND
The frontend is ready for release review only when:
All in-scope pages are implemented and wired to the real API contracts.
No primary button is inert and no form silently fakes success.
All long-running tasks show truthful server-backed states or honest indeterminate states.
Authenticated routes and data operations respect the backend's ownership model.
Loading, empty, validation, offline, failure, retry, deleted-item and unsupported-capability states are covered.
Desktop, tablet and mobile layouts work at the specified viewport widths with no horizontal overflow or inaccessible controls.
Both themes are polished, persisted, readable and contrast-checked.
Keyboard navigation, focus indicators, accessible labels, dialogs, upload and job announcements are tested.
Frontend unit/component tests, a main-path E2E test, lint/type checks where configured, and a production build have been run with results recorded.
No fake production activity, sample users, invented metrics, false status indicators, hard-coded successful AI responses, or visible developer placeholders remain.
